  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nLatest surface and radar obs show a broad area of light rain\nimpinging on northwest Wisconsin this afternoon where mid-level \nshortwave and weak moisture convergence reside. Scattered showers \nshould make it to northeast Wisconsin by this evening, though any \nQPF we manage to squeeze out should top off at a few hundredths of\nan inch as precip encounters a wall of dry air. Signal then once \nagain exists for patchy fog development late tonight in the wake \nof the showers as winds decouple and remnant moisture gets trapped\nunder near-surface inversion. Have a sneaking suspicion that fog \nmay end up overperforming similar to this morning, so will bear \nmonitoring.\n\nModels continue to back off on rain chances over the weekend as\nHudson Bay high dominates the synoptic pattern. As such, PoPs\nare held down to around 10 to 20% mainly over central and north-\ncentral Wisconsin where models show the greatest chance for \nshowers. Strung out surface low then ejects off into northern \nCanada early Friday morning, resulting in showers splitting to our\nnorth and south.\n\nNext chances for widespread stratiform rain remain on track for \nthe middle of next week as low pressure organizes over the central\nPlains and deepening trough passes over the region. Though\nensembles still diverge on exact timing of precip, consensus shows\nPWATs reaching near 90th percentile relative to climo, increasing\nconfidence in at least a soaking rainfall. Probabilistic guidance\ncurrently shows a 40 to 50% signal for receiving one inch of\nrainfall, though trends will need to continue to be monitored.\nOtherwise, seasonal temperatures in the 60s to low 70s prevail \nfor the extended.",
